TITLE:: ParaEQView
summary:: the response of an eq and its seven dots: the eq's display, the one you play
categories:: Libraries>ParaEQ
related:: Classes/ParaEQ, Classes/ParaEQPanel, Classes/ParaEQSpectrum

DESCRIPTION::
One plane, frequency along (log, 20 Hz to 20 kHz) and gain up. On it, each band's
lift or cut is a fill in the band's colour, each cut that is in a silver veil over
what it takes away, and the sum of them all - what you hear - one ivory line. Seven
dots to take hold of: the five bands, numbered 1 to 5 low to high, in their colours,
and the two cuts, a silver dot at each end marked with its own shape - the low cut
climbing out of the lows, the high cut falling into the highs. A cut that is out is
a ring: still there to take hold of, and saying at a glance that it does nothing.

Behind them, when the view has been given a link::Classes/ParaEQSpectrum::, the
spectrum of what leaves the eq, on a level scale of its own. A strip along the top
names the selected dot in its colour; its right end reads the pointer - the
frequency, the nearest note as Tidal says it (note 0 is code::c5::, 261.6 Hz, so
code::a5:: is 440 and code::e2:: 41.2), the region and, with the spectrum on, the
level there. Under the Hz the Cs of Tidal's octaves, code::c2:: to code::c9::. This
is the middle of link::Classes/ParaEQPanel::; a window that wants only the curve can
make one on its own.

code::
(
w = Window("curve", Rect(100, 100, 700, 300)).front;
v = ParaEQView(w, w.view.bounds.insetBy(8));
v.eq = ParaEQ.new.preset_(\warm);
)
::

definitionList::
## drag a dot || a band: frequency and gain. A cut: where it cuts, and up for a corner that rings (its res - flat on the 0 line)
## press anywhere || takes the nearest dot without moving it, so a dot never has to be hit exactly; a drag then moves it
## shift || fine: a tenth of the movement, dragging or on the wheel
## the wheel on a dot || a band's width, up wider - a cut's slope, a step a notch. Off any dot it works the selected one
## cmd + drag up / down || the selected band's width, or the selected cut's res, from wherever you press. code::ctrl:: or code::alt:: do the same
## double click a dot || a band back to 0 dB - a cut in or out
## right click a dot || a menu: back to 0 dB, back to where it starts, type a frequency - on a cut: in, its slope, a flat corner
::

Every drag is relative - from where the dot was, not where the mouse landed - so
nothing jumps on the first pixel. While a dot is dragged its values stand beside it
in accent and in the hint bar. A band pushed past the edge of the plane (see
link::#-range::) waits there with an arrow; nothing about it changes.

The curve is worked out from the same coefficients the server runs, see
link::Classes/ParaEQ#*coeffs::, and kept between redraws: with an analyser running
the view redraws fifteen times a second, and seven filters over a few hundred
frequencies is not work to do that often.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: parent
ARGUMENT:: bounds

METHOD:: grabRadius
How near a dot the pointer has to be to count as on it: the double click, the
wheel, the hover. 20.

METHOD:: widthPerPixel
How much of the width one pixel of a cmd drag is worth. 1.02.

METHOD:: lastRange
The height the next view opens at, in dB either side of flat: the last one chosen.

METHOD:: ranges
The heights a view can show: code::[6, 12, 24]::.

METHOD:: freqSnap
METHOD:: shelfQSnap
METHOD:: peakQSnap
What shift snapped to before it meant fine. Kept for anyone who reads them.

METHOD:: isWidthMod
Which modifier keys mean "width, not position": cmd on macOS, ctrl elsewhere, alt
everywhere (QtCollider hands every platform Cocoa's bits).

METHOD:: handleLabel
What a handle is called in a word: the bands numbered 1 to 5, the cuts code::"/"::
and code::"\\"::.

METHOD:: font
METHOD:: boldFont
The 9 pt fonts the dots and axes use.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: view
The link::Classes/UserView::.

METHOD:: eq
The link::Classes/ParaEQ:: drawn, or nil for an empty plane.

METHOD:: spectrum
A link::Classes/ParaEQSpectrum::, or nil for the curve alone.

METHOD:: selected
The handle being worked on, 0 to link::Classes/ParaEQ#*numHandles:: - 1. It lives
here and nowhere else, so setting it from outside reaches a panel's cards too.

METHOD:: action
Called with the view and code::\select:: when the selection changes, and with
code::\range:: when the height does.

METHOD:: hoverAction
Called with the view and the handle under the pointer, or nil, as it changes: a
panel lights that band's number and knobs.

METHOD:: editAction
Called with the view and a handle when its menu asks to type a frequency.

METHOD:: hot
A handle to ring because its knob is under the pointer, or nil.

METHOD:: hover
The handle under the pointer, or nil.

METHOD:: range
How many dB either side of flat the plane shows: 6, 12 or 24. Only the view: the
bands keep their gains.

METHOD:: hints
A hint bar (link::Classes/FxHintBar::, the module family's ModuleHintBar ...) the
view writes to itself as the pointer moves and a dot is dragged. A bar's own
code::attach:: would take over the pointer the view follows, so do not attach this
view to it.

METHOD:: hint
What the hint bar says with the pointer here.
ARGUMENT:: x
ARGUMENT:: y
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::

METHOD:: isDragging
Whether a dot is being dragged.

METHOD:: refresh
